Ladybird serializes a runtime message as JSON in the sender’s realm,
hands it to the UI process with a request id, and on the receiving
page runs every onMessage listener with (message, sender,
sendResponse), where sendResponse, a returned true plus a later
sendResponse, or a returned promise answers, the first answer wins,
and a plain returned value is ignored. WebKit does the same in
WebExtensionAPIRuntime::sendMessage (JSON through
Messages::WebExtensionContext::RuntimeSendMessage) and
WebExtensionContextProxy::dispatchRuntimeMessageEvent(), whose
listeners get a sendResponse callback and may return true or a
promise; its WebExtensionCallbackHandler resolves the first reply and
replies null when every listener finishes without one. Blink’s
OneTimeMessageHandler (extensions/renderer/api/messaging/) opens a
channel through MessageService::OpenChannelToExtension(), passes a
sendResponse to each listener, keeps the port open for a true return
value or a returned promise (MV3), and closes it with no response
when all listeners return without one; a returned non-promise value
is discarded. Gecko’s Messenger.sendRuntimeMessage() in
ExtensionChild.sys.mjs serializes with a StructuredCloneHolder and
casts the message through ProxyMessenger.recvRuntimeMessage() to
every context; its recvRuntimeMessage() runs the listeners, treats a
returned promise or a true-plus-sendResponse as the reply, and
resolves with the first defined one. Ladybird keeps the reply
collector as a GC cell so a sendResponse that arrives after the
document died does nothing.

Ladybird’s sendMessage() sorts out its arguments the way Blink’s
RuntimeHooksDelegate::HandleSendMessage() does, and so does Gecko’s
parseBonkersArgs(), except that it reads an ambiguous two-argument
call as a message and options. Ladybird accepts the includeTlsChannelId
option, which does nothing, as Blink does; Gecko accepts only empty
options, and WebKit ignores them.

runtime.lastError holds an error only while the callback of a failed
call runs, as in Blink’s APIRequestHandler::AsyncResultHandler::
ResolveRequest(), Gecko’s BaseContext::withLastError(), and WebKit’s
WebExtensionAPIRuntimeBase::reportError().

A context that goes away drops its pending calls and declines the
answers it owed. Blink’s ExtensionMessagePort::Prune() removes a
disconnected frame’s receivers and their pending responses, and Gecko’s
recvConduitClosed() closes the context’s port. WebKit’s one-shot path
ties replies to a ReplyCallbackAggregator its callbacks hold, rather
than to a per-document map.
